Conclusions supported by the measurements
-----------------------------------------
1. Input/map-range failure is not the primary cause. Median outside-map rejects are zero in every stair-height band.
2. A* exhausts all reachable nodes because inflated stair/landing voxels disconnect the searched reference-height manifold. Every parsed stair A* failure is OPEN_SET_EMPTY, not SEARCH_TIMEOUT.
3. Fixed-plane A* also places early control points in occupied space. Adding a vertical band reduces "robot inside obstacle" from 113 to 6 and rebound-direction failures from 106 to 12.
4. A vertical band alone is insufficient. It increases the final unsafe/infeasible count from 16 to 38 because a collision-free voxel path may contain vertical motion that the B-spline smoothing/dynamic constraints cannot safely reproduce.
5. The approximately +0.28 m start-Z correction is present in both runs. It is a contributing alignment sensitivity, but it does not explain the different outcomes between the two A* variants.

Primary root cause
------------------
The planner uses an aerial-style inflated 3-D occupancy representation for a ground robot on stairs, but its front end searches either one linear Z plane or a narrow unconstrained Z band. Neither representation models a traversable stair surface and the front/rear body supports at different terrain heights. The front end therefore sees false topological blockage; when the vertical band finds a geometric detour, the backend often rejects its vertical profile.
